---

<SiteLayout metadata={pageSeo["/ideas/the-shortage-that-cannot-hide"]}>
	<main id="main" class="ideas-post">
		<article aria-labelledby="post-title">
			<header class="opening reading">
				<p class="eyebrow">3Back point of view</p>
				<h1 id="post-title">The Shortage That Cannot Hide</h1>
				<p class="lead">What the skilled trades reveal about domain expertise in other kinds of work</p>
			</header>

			<figure class="post-figure reading">
				<Image
					src={shortageImage}
					alt="A technician kneels at a server rack and seats an orange cable under a dense overhead run of labeled cables, including a tag that reads DO NOT UNPLUG."
					widths={[480, 760, 1200]}
					sizes="(max-width: 808px) calc(100vw - 48px), 760px"
					loading="eager"
				/>
			</figure>

			<section class="reading prose" aria-label="The shortage that cannot hide">
				<p>America has a skilled-labor problem.</p>
				<p>A new report from the Alliance for America's Skilled Trades estimates that the United States will need to fill roughly 1.7 million skilled-trades openings each year through 2035. Current training programs are producing only about 55 workers for every 100 needed. Developing domain expertise in the skilled trades takes roughly five years of mentorship and practical work experience.</p>
				<p>In the skilled trades, a shortage of domain expertise is impossible to miss. Someone has to build the data centers, maintain the machinery, wire the electrical systems, install the cooling, repair the vehicles, and keep the physical infrastructure underneath the economy running. If that domain expertise is missing, the consequences rapidly show up on a “punch list” for corrective action.</p>
				<p>But what happens when missing domain expertise is harder to inspect and its consequences do not readily produce a “punch list” (bugs/defects)?</p>
				<blockquote>The shortage of domain expertise can easily hide in other kinds of work. In the skilled trades, it cannot.</blockquote>
			</section>

			<section class="reading prose">
				<h2>The work eventually tells you</h2>
				<p>Skilled trades have an unforgiving characteristic: much of the work is readily evaluated against well-established standards that can be used to inspect and accept it. Somebody has to perform the work against those standards. The electrician has to wire the panel. The machinist has to make the part. The mechanic has to diagnose the failure. The carpenter has to build something that fits, carries the load, and stays standing.</p>
				<p>Education matters in all of those fields. Technical knowledge matters. Training matters. But none of them can permanently substitute for the ability to perform against actual work conditions.</p>
				<p>Standards alone do not produce good work. Pride and craftsmanship matter because a professional does not wait for an inspection to care about quality.</p>
				<p>Professionals make three distinct commitments:</p>
				<ol>
					<li>Approach problems constructively.</li>
					<li>Apply serious effort.</li>
					<li>Stand behind the work and correct it when it falls short.</li>
				</ol>
				<p>Those commitments do not substitute for expertise. They shape how someone responds to standards, feedback, and correction while expertise develops.</p>
				<p>A person who knows the terminology but cannot do the work becomes visible. A training program that produces graduates without sufficient capability eventually reveals the gap. An organization that lacks enough people capable of performing the work eventually feels the consequences directly.</p>
				<p>In the skilled trades, those consequences commonly arrive on a short clock. Standards, inspections, and observable results make capability visible before weak judgment travels far.</p>
				<p>New and rapidly changing domains create a different problem. Innovation can produce new areas of practice faster than standards, inspection methods, and experienced judgment can mature. The work still answers back, but the evidence may arrive late, appear somewhere else in the system, or be difficult to trace to the judgment that produced it.</p>
				<blockquote>The domain eventually answers back. The risk is how long the answer takes.</blockquote>
				<p>When work is harder to inspect, its standards are still emerging, and its consequences are delayed or distributed, a shortage of domain expertise can remain hidden.</p>
			</section>

			<section class="reading prose">
				<h2>Headcount can hide a shortage of expertise</h2>
				<p>An organization can fill every role and still lack depth in domain expertise. Headcount shows coverage, but it does not show capability. How would leadership know whether the organization has enough domain expertise?</p>
				<p>In a mature domain, capability can be vetted through observed performance against established standards. But when standards are still emerging and consequences arrive late, that evidence is harder to obtain.</p>
				<p>Cause and effect in knowledge work can be separated by weeks, months, or years. Responsibility can be distributed across teams, managers, vendors, systems, and functions. A poor decision can be absorbed by the organization long before anyone identifies the judgment that produced it.</p>
				<p>Under those conditions, visible proxies can make organizational capability appear deeper than it is:</p>
				<ol>
					<li><strong>Fluency without understanding.</strong> Someone can know the vocabulary of product development without understanding the product, the customer, or the market.</li>
					<li><strong>Method knowledge without work knowledge.</strong> Someone can understand a management framework while knowing very little about the work of the people expected to use it.</li>
					<li><strong>Authority ahead of judgment.</strong> Someone can earn a credential, satisfy a hiring requirement, and gain authority to apply a method before experience has taught them when it fits, when it does not, or what its application is doing to the work.</li>
				</ol>
				<p>Each can provide evidence of preparation. None, by itself, demonstrates domain expertise or professional judgment under actual conditions.</p>
				<p>An organization may not recognize the shortage until delayed consequences make it visible. By then, weak judgment may already have compromised the capability the organization needs.</p>
				<p>Leadership then has to vet and rebuild that capability. But when performance remains difficult to inspect, the organization reaches for the evidence it can readily see.</p>
				<blockquote>It reaches for credentials because credentials are simple to verify. Capability is not.</blockquote>
			</section>

			<section class="reading prose">
				<h2>Credentials became proxies for expertise</h2>
				<p>This is not an argument against universities.</p>
				<p>Education can establish knowledge, expose people to ideas they would not otherwise encounter, develop analytical ability, and prepare someone to enter a profession.</p>
				<p>The problem begins when evidence of education is treated as evidence of expertise.</p>
				<p>Harvard Business School research on degree inflation documented employers adding degree requirements to jobs that historically had not required them, often using the degree as a proxy for qualities employers wanted but were not directly observing.</p>
				<blockquote>Completion becomes qualification.<br />Qualification becomes capability.<br />Capability becomes authority.</blockquote>
				<p>Each transition can be justified. None is automatic. The mistake is allowing the credential to end the inquiry into capability.</p>
				<p>A sanitized learning environment can isolate a concept and make it easier to understand. Real work does the opposite. It introduces conflicting objectives, incomplete information, customers, history, technical debt, budgets, regulation, personalities, deadlines, and consequences.</p>
				<p>Those conditions are not interference surrounding professional capability. They are where professional capability develops.</p>
				<blockquote>The trades did not invent the distinction between education and professional capability. They simply operate in environments where the distinction is difficult to conceal.</blockquote>
			</section>

			<section class="reading prose">
				<h2>A judgment deficit does more damage than a visible shortage</h2>
				<p>A visible shortage slows or stops the work. A hidden deficit in professional judgment may not. The organization keeps moving while weak decisions accumulate across process, software, staffing, and infrastructure.</p>
				<p>By the time the deficit becomes visible, the problem may no longer belong to one person or one decision. It has become a structural part of how the organization operates.</p>
				<p>Consider a compounding pharmacy beginning to scale. At the compounding bench, domain expertise is difficult to fake. Formulations, concentrations, procedures, and controls have to be correct because errors involving contamination or incorrect potency can cause serious patient harm or death. The standards are explicit, the work can be inspected, and the repercussions are clear.</p>
				<p>Now move one layer away from the bench and add an infusion of capital with an expectation of rapid growth. The company hires. Management layers appear. New systems are commissioned. Workflows are standardized. Software is added. Procedures are formalized so that more people can be brought into the organization faster.</p>
				<p>Much of that is necessary.</p>
				<p>But suppose a question that was once resolved by an experienced pharmacist walking to the bench is encoded into the new system as a required field, routed to a queue, and followed by an approval step. On paper, the process looks cleaner. It creates a record and appears more controlled.</p>
				<p>It goes live. Nothing breaks.</p>
				<p>Then the exceptions start. The people closest to the work create workarounds. One approval becomes two because the first no longer carries enough context. A dashboard appears to track the queue. Each addition solves the problem immediately in front of it, and each becomes another dependency the next decision has to respect.</p>
				<p>The organization is now operating through the judgment deficit.</p>
				<blockquote>The most dangerous work is not always work that fails. It is work that survives long enough to become structural.</blockquote>
				<p>An infusion of capital can force an innovation into scale before the organization has developed the expertise and judgment to govern it. Staff, systems, and processes can grow quickly. Expertise and judgment cannot be accelerated at the same rate.</p>
				<p>Research on startup scaling describes the hazard as commitment risk: resources are committed before enough learning has occurred.</p>
				<p>The point is not that investment causes poor execution. It is that capital can remove a constraint that once forced the organization to learn before it multiplied.</p>
				<blockquote>You can add bodies, systems, and AI-driven speed faster than you can develop expertise and judgment.</blockquote>
			</section>

			<section class="reading prose">
				<h2>The organization starts hiding the evidence from itself</h2>
				<p>Once a judgment deficit becomes structural, evidence of it has to travel through the same organization that produced it.</p>
				<p>Someone has already been hired, promoted, or given authority on the assumption that they are capable. Contrary evidence now reflects not only on that person, but also on the managers who selected them, promoted them, or built a function around them.</p>
				<p>That does not require deliberate deception. Organizations provide plenty of more comfortable explanations: the team needs more support, the process needs refinement, another department created the dependency, the organization needs better alignment, the work is unusually complex, or the initiative needs more time.</p>
				<p>Any one of those explanations may be true. They can also sanitize the underlying signal.</p>
				<p>What began as insufficient judgment close to the work can arrive at senior leadership as an alignment problem, a transformation problem, a communication problem, or simply the unavoidable complexity of the business.</p>
				<p>By then, the original question may have disappeared:</p>
				<blockquote>Do the people making these decisions possess enough expertise in this domain to make them well?</blockquote>
				<p>The delay does not merely postpone recognition.</p>
				<blockquote>It gives the damage time to compound.</blockquote>
			</section>

			<section class="reading prose">
				<h2>AI widens the gap</h2>
				<p>AI did not create the judgment deficit. It increases the speed and volume at which weak judgment can become structural.</p>
				<p>A person with limited domain expertise can now produce credible-looking analysis, code, plans, documentation, recommendations, process designs, and architectural alternatives at a level and volume that previously required far more accumulated experience.</p>
				<p>Production becomes cheaper. Selection does not.</p>
				<p>Every generated artifact still requires someone to decide what belongs, what should be discarded, which assumption is dangerous, and what the organization can safely absorb. If that judgment is weak, AI can add code, process, structure, and dependencies faster than the organization can understand what has been added.</p>
				<blockquote>The question is no longer simply whether the work can be produced. It is whether someone with enough domain expertise is governing what gets produced.</blockquote>
			</section>

			<section class="reading prose">
				<h2>Capability develops by doing the work</h2>
				<p>People develop capability by doing real work, seeing what happens, and correcting their judgment.</p>
				<p>Education provides foundational knowledge. Training introduces techniques and prepares people to enter a domain. Neither can permanently substitute for experience under actual work conditions.</p>
				<p>Apprenticeship preserves a sequence that organizations routinely say they value and then abandon under pressure:</p>
				<ol>
					<li>Build foundational knowledge. Give people the concepts and understanding needed to enter the work intelligently.</li>
					<li>Put that knowledge to work. Let people operate against actual demand, constraints, customers, systems, and consequences alongside people with deeper experience.</li>
					<li>Make judgment observable. Let them make decisions, see the effects, compare expectation with reality, and correct their interpretation.</li>
					<li>Expand authority as capability becomes evident. Responsibility grows because judgment has been demonstrated in the work, not simply because another program was completed.</li>
				</ol>
				<p>The point is not that every profession needs a traditional trade apprenticeship. The point is the sequence.</p>
				<p>When hiring outruns development, authority outruns evidence. The organization needs people now, so the best available proxy becomes proof and the organization keeps moving.</p>
				<p>That can accelerate growth. It can also accelerate everything the organization will eventually have to untangle.</p>
			</section>

			<section class="reading prose">
				<h2>The question for leaders</h2>
				<p>The skilled-trades shortage deserves attention on its own. Executives should also pay attention to what it reveals.</p>
				<p>The trades operate close enough to consequence that weak capability is difficult to conceal. In knowledge work, fluency and credentials can be mistaken for capability and judgment that the work has never demonstrated. Activity can look like progress, complexity can look like sophistication, and for a while everything can look fine.</p>
				<p>Leadership cannot wait for delayed consequences to reveal where the organization lacks expertise. It has to ask:</p>
				<ol>
					<li>Where are we relying on evidence that someone was prepared instead of evidence that they can perform?</li>
					<li>Where has authority grown faster than demonstrated judgment?</li>
					<li>Where are weak standards, limited inspectability, or delayed consequences allowing a deficit in expertise to remain hidden?</li>
				</ol>
				<blockquote>Where the work will not make capability visible quickly, leadership has to make it observable.</blockquote>
			</section>

